  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Thursday: Strong high pressure ridging extending \nup through the Intermountain West from the south-central Plains \nwill keep the region mainly dry and increasingly hot for much \nthis week. 850mb temps climbing into the mid and upper 20s (C) \nwill cause the heat to likely peak in the Wed/Thu timeframe. \nWidespread moderate HeatRisk with pockets of major HeatRisk is \nexpected as early as Tuesday, though by midweek the major \nHeatRisk area will expand to encompass much of the Spokane/Coeur\nd'Alene area and west across the Columbia Basin and Waterville \nPlateau. There still remains some uncertainty in how high cloud \ncover and wildfire smoke will impact temps, though ensemble \nguidance continues to indicate high confidence in max temps \nexceeding the upper 90s or 100s over a sizable footprint of this\nregion; NBM probabilities for MaxT>=100F max out at around \n60%-90% Wednesday. As such, heat headlines will eventually be \nneeded once we get a bit closer and the finer details come into \nfocus. The aforementioned cloud cover will be in place as \nmonsoonal moisture slowly advects into the region from the south\nthis week. This moisture plume will likely make it into \nsouthern WA and central ID, but will begin to encounter \nnortherly mid level flow coming down from British Columbia and \nAlberta. Early in the week, in additional to some added clouds, \nthere could be a few sprinkles/virga across Palouse and areas \nfurther south Monday though model soundings indicate plenty of \ndry air beneath 12kft so not expecting any wetting rains. Later \nin the week, a Pacific trough will attempt to slide into the \nNorthwest as the ridge potentially breaks down. Will need to \ncontinue monitoring trends as this added lifting mechanism may \nlead to increasing chances for convection Wednesday into \nThursday.\n\nFriday through Sunday: Ensemble guidance spread remains \nunusually low in the general evolution of the longwave pattern \nnext weekend. A trough pivoting southeastward from Alaska will \nphase with a shortwave off the Pacific NW coast, with the \naggregate trough sliding northeastward towards the crest of the \nstrong high pressure ridge across the desert southwest. This \nwill result in some relief from the heat seen earlier in the \nweek, though the ridge weakening may lead to elevated fire \nweather conditions with decreasing humidity and breezy winds. \nLong range ensembles do indicate decreasing PWAT anomalies which\nsignals a decreased threat for thunderstorms, though can't \nquite rule out some isolated convection from Thursday rolling to\nFriday.",
